        "slug": "bot-protection",
        "title": "Bot protection and a night hosting outage",
        "summary": "Bots ate the paid daily limit of receipt checks. I closed the service to bots, described the server as code and added monitoring that caught a night hosting outage.",
        "relatedWorkSlug": "anti-flood-mitigation",
        "stack": "Docker, Ansible, nginx, fail2ban, nftables, captcha, request queue, Prometheus, Grafana",
        "html": "<pre><code class=\"language-text\">users and bots\n  -&gt; captcha, limits, queue\n  -&gt; nginx and fail2ban\n  -&gt; nftables\n  -&gt; app in a closed Docker network\n  -&gt; daily limit used by real people only\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Problem</h2>\n<p>The service checks receipts through an external provider. The provider gives a fixed number of checks per day.</p>\n<p>From December 2025, bots used up this limit. Real users got errors until the next day. The server was set up by hand, and there was no test copy.</p>\n<h2>What I did</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Recorded each request with its source. This showed how the bots worked.</li>\n<li>Added a captcha, a per-user request limit and a queue. They stop bots before the paid check.</li>\n<li>Added automatic blocks for sources that send too many requests.</li>\n<li>Packed the service in Docker. Only the website is open; the database and internal parts are in a closed network.</li>\n<li>Described the server with Ansible roles and in March 2026 built a test environment.</li>\n<li>In February 2026 connected the service to Prometheus and Grafana: an alert when the site is down for more than 5 minutes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Result</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The bot attacks on the paid limit stopped.</li>\n<li>Real users can check receipts again.</li>\n<li>The server and a test copy can be built from code.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Night outage, 2 November 2026</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>02:41 Moscow time the site stopped answering. Short drops of 1-2 minutes came first; they did not trigger an alert.</li>\n<li>02:46 a critical alert reached the team chat.</li>\n<li>09:36-10:00 I checked DNS from three public services, the domain payment, and the server directly from five points in four countries.</li>\n<li>Cause: a full outage at the hosting provider, both its DNS and its servers. Our setup had no error.</li>\n<li>I found a hidden risk: domain, DNS and server at one provider. My plan: move DNS, backups and a reserve server to other providers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>More</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/assets/cases/en/01-bot-protection.pdf\">Bot protection case, PDF</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/assets/cases/en/04-night-outage.pdf\">Night outage case, PDF</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>I do not publish exact rules, thresholds, addresses or logs.</p>"
